# LF everywhere; Windows contributors get consistent bytes (same rationale as Tinker).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures and any future golden images are binary - never normalized.
#
# The JPEG 2000 pair is named explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`,
# which decides by looking for a NUL in the first 8 KB. Every fixture in the
# tree today happens to have one, but a PGM of an evenly mid-grey image has
# none - and if such a file ever contains the two bytes 0D 0A, committing it
# from Windows would rewrite them and change the picture.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jp2 binary
*.pgm binary

# The reviewed goldens (design/render-verification.md milestone 6). Binary PPMs
# whose `#` comment header carries the review — family, clause, reviewer, date —
# so a normalised line ending inside one is both a different picture and a
# damaged record of who read it.
*.ppm binary

# The XPS corpus (gap 30 milestone 1, and Tier 4's second producer). These are
# OPC packages -- ZIPs -- so `text=auto` would leave them alone anyway; they are
# named because the reason not to touch them is stronger than "it found a NUL".
#
# The eight Microsoft packages are not regenerable byte for byte, since both
# serialisers mint a fresh GUID per resource part, so a normalised line ending
# inside a stored part would break its CRC-32 and there would be no way back.
# The five Ghostscript packages *are* regenerable -- and they need this more,
# not less. Every one of their thirty entries is **stored**, where WPF deflates
# all but one, so a normalised byte anywhere in any of them breaks a CRC-32;
# what regenerability buys is that the damage would be repairable, not that it
# would be absent, and a corpus that has to be rebuilt to be trusted is not one
# git is holding correctly.
*.xps binary
*.oxps binary

# The EPUB corpus (gap 31 milestone 1). OCF containers -- ZIPs again -- named
# for the same reason and one more of its own. `mimetype` is a **stored** entry
# holding exactly twenty ASCII bytes, and OCF 3.3 §4.3.2 requires those twenty
# and no others; a normalised line ending anywhere in a stored entry breaks its
# CRC-32, and there would be no way back, because neither producer is
# reproducible byte for byte -- pandoc and calibre each mint a fresh UUID for
# the package document's `dc:identifier` on every run.
*.epub binary

# The comic corpus (tier 4). Four container formats holding one set of pages,
# named for the ZIP reason above and one that is sharper for two of them: a
# `.cbz` written by 7-Zip or WinRAR **stores** most of its entries, so there is
# more raw picture sitting outside a compressed stream here than anywhere else
# in the tree, and a normalised line ending in any of it breaks a CRC-32 with
# no way back. The RAR, 7z and tar are refused by this build today and are
# committed so that the decoders which read them have something to be held to;
# a fixture nobody can read yet is exactly the one nothing else would protect.
*.cbz binary
*.cbr binary
*.cb7 binary
*.cbt binary

# Fuzz seeds are bytes chosen for what they make a parser do; a normalized
# line ending is a different input, and several of them have no extension.
fuzz/corpus/** binary

# Adobe's cmap-resources, vendored verbatim (THIRDPARTY.md). `-text` keeps the
# bytes exactly as published, because BSD-3-Clause asks a redistribution to
# retain the notice and normalizing line endings is still a modification.
# `linguist-vendored` collapses eight megabytes of PostScript out of the diff
# a reviewer has to read, and out of the repository's language statistics.
crates/tinker-pdf-font/data/** -text linguist-vendored

# The UCD (gap 31 milestone 7, THIRDPARTY.md). `-text` for cmap-resources'
# reason and one more of its own: the published files are CRLF, the licence
# asks a redistribution to carry the notice, and normalising line endings is
# still a modification. `linguist-vendored` keeps four megabytes of Unicode
# out of the diff a reviewer reads and out of the language statistics.
crates/tinker-pdf-layout/data/** -text linguist-vendored

# Adobe's aots conformance corpus (design/shaping.md milestone 1,
# THIRDPARTY.md). `-text` for the two reasons above and one that is sharper
# here: a `.otf` is a table directory whose entries carry byte offsets and
# lengths, so a single rewritten line ending moves every table after it and
# the face stops parsing. Nothing in this tree names `*.otf` binary, and
# `text=auto` decides by looking for a NUL in the first 8 KB — which these
# happen to have and which is not a property worth relying on.
# `linguist-vendored` keeps a megabyte of test faces out of the diff a
# reviewer reads and out of the language statistics.
crates/tinker-pdf-shape/data/** -text linguist-vendored
# RFC 7932's static dictionary (THIRDPARTY.md). `-text` because it is binary --
# the RFC publishes it as hexadecimal and this is that dump decoded, so it is
# 122 784 bytes of arbitrary values with no line structure to normalise, and
# the CRC-32 the RFC publishes is what a test checks it against.
# `linguist-vendored` keeps it out of the diff a reviewer reads and out of the
# repository's language statistics.
crates/tinker-pdf-filters/data/** -text linguist-vendored

# The committed Brotli reference streams (`tests/brotli_vectors.rs`). Bytes an
# encoder emitted once, on a named day; a normalised line ending inside one is
# a different stream, and several have no structure that would survive it.
crates/tinker-pdf-filters/tests/brotli/** binary

# The WOFF corpus (tier 4, `crates/tinker-pdf-font/tests/woff/`). Two faces and
# five packings of them, named binary for a reason that is sharper here than
# for any other fixture in this tree.
#
# A WOFF 1.0 is a table directory of byte offsets over per-table zlib streams,
# and a WOFF 2.0 is a table directory of byte offsets over one Brotli stream:
# a rewritten line ending anywhere in either moves every offset after it, and
# the file stops being a font rather than becoming a slightly different one.
# The `.ttf` pair beside them is the same argument -- an sfnt directory carries
# offsets and lengths, which is already why `crates/tinker-pdf-shape/data/**`
# is spelled out.
#
# What is different here is that these *are* regenerable, byte for byte, by
# `tests/woff/make-fixtures.py` -- and that argues for this line rather than
# against it, exactly as it does for the five Ghostscript packages above. What
# regenerability buys is that damage would be repairable, not that it would be
# absent; and repairing it needs fontTools, Node and two npm packages that no
# CI job here installs, so in practice a normalised byte would be found by a
# test failure and fixed by nobody.
#
# `*.woff` and `*.woff2` are named rather than left to `text=auto`, which
# decides by looking for a NUL in the first 8 KB. A Brotli stream of a small
# font is a few hundred bytes of entropy that may well have none.
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
crates/tinker-pdf-font/tests/woff/*.ttf binary
